{
  "dom_id": "step-q142999",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/c61b5aec221dd257c507fdaa8f2a04f0f29b158a6ea93afd38a310fc1150669d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ST}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TP}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{SQ}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$",
  "worked_solution": "We want to find a \"path\" from $S$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=\\overset{\\to }{ST}+\\overset{\\to }{TP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/abd73b231ee79396e64c018ccfaed752efff4bedc053b1d9977761c283a3b994.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{ST}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TP}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=-a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/c560ab74e5d87c51adb19c287b1e75aa2ee112170f3e6361e1b5bc65c182dbda.png)\n\n   \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{ST}+\\overset{\\to }{TP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =b+d-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
